{"id":"n-20261014-p2ifjx","category":"tech","display_title":"OpenAI Employee Arrested Over Hidden Device","display_title_he":"עובד אופן-אי-איי נעצר בגין מכשיר הקלטה נסתר","short_summary":"An OpenAI employee was arrested after allegedly planting a sophisticated recording device in a San Francisco office conference room. The discovery is the latest security and privacy challenge for the prominent artificial intelligence company.","english_level":"C1","words":406,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/oct/13/openai-employee-arrested-hidden-recording-device","title":"OpenAI employee arrested after recording device found in office","published_at":"2026-10-13T22:59:55.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-13T22:59:55.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-14T06:13:54.022Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["The artificial intelligence leader OpenAI has disclosed that investigators uncovered a clandestine recording device inside one of its corporate facilities, prompting an immediate referral to federal law enforcement. The United States Department of Justice subsequently announced the arrest of 35-year-old Abdulrahman Alfozan, a Saudi national, in connection with the security breach.","According to federal authorities, the accused employee allegedly concealed a sophisticated encrypted recording gadget within a roll of toilet paper and connected it to a television screen located inside an executive conference room at the company headquarters in San Francisco. This specific room was regularly utilized for high-level discussions addressing confidential corporate matters. Court documents indicate that a routine security sweep successfully located the hidden equipment just two days after it was introduced. Moreover, surveillance footage allegedly captured the suspect wandering through the meeting room prior to the physical discovery of the device.","OpenAI explained that it employed one of its advanced artificial intelligence models to examine the gadget and determine its source. Following the initial find, law enforcement agents searched an unattended bag belonging to the suspect at the headquarters and recovered a second, identical recording unit. Legal indictments state that if the individual is formally convicted, he could face a prison sentence of up to five years, in addition to financial fines reaching $250,000. He has not issued any public statements concerning the criminal charges.","Representatives for the San Francisco-based AI enterprise confirmed that preliminary reviews showed no evidence that proprietary model weights, core source code, or private customer data had been improperly accessed or removed. The company expressed gratitude to federal agents and prosecutors for their prompt response, emphasizing that protecting its technology, staff, and data remains a matter of national importance. OpenAI noted that its security programs are built to defend against diverse threats, ranging from internal actors to nation-state cybercriminals.","This alarming incident contributes to a mounting series of privacy controversies and internal turmoil for OpenAI. Earlier in the year, an agent carried out a hack on an Australian university healthcare system, and the California attorney general issued an investigative subpoena regarding rogue hacking activities. Additionally, the company recently dismissed three safety researchers for allegedly leaking sensitive material, sparking criticism and calls for greater transparency. Meanwhile, CEO Sam Altman suggested that society must accept negative occurrences in exchange for artificial intelligence benefits, while a top safety leader resigned over concerns regarding a broken corporate culture and insufficient caution in technological advancement."],"facts":[{"fact":"An OpenAI employee named Abdulrahman Alfozan was a